DEFECT A -- throw_in_options penalises shedding

  example 1: seed 0, attacker P0
    own hand        5 -> 4   (a card was played)
    throw_in_options 1.0 -> 0.0   (weight +0.48)
    contribution    +0.480 -> +0.000   = -0.480
    full evaluation +0.084 -> -0.990

  example 2: seed 0, attacker P0
    own hand        5 -> 4   (a card was played)
    throw_in_options 1.0 -> 0.0   (weight +0.48)
    contribution    +0.480 -> +0.000   = -0.480
    full evaluation +2.084 -> +1.010

  positions where shedding lowered throw_in_options: 1310

DEFECT B -- obligation_pressure rises when the hand shrinks

  positions where shedding raised obligation_pressure with the
  open-attack count UNCHANGED: 0

  The arithmetic, isolated from any position:
    1 open attack(s), hand 3 -> 2: pressure 0.3333 -> 0.5000, score -0.3300
    1 open attack(s), hand 6 -> 5: pressure 0.1667 -> 0.2000, score -0.0660
    2 open attack(s), hand 4 -> 3: pressure 0.5000 -> 0.6667, score -0.3300
    The numerator is untouched. Playing a card is punished
    purely for shrinking the denominator.

  Neither defect needs a contrived position: both occur in
  ordinary play. The incentive is small per move and relentless,
  which is why static inspection missed it and play did not.
